<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mexican War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mexican War of Independence was the armed conflict of 1810–1821 that ended Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. It began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>'s call to revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a> on 16 September 1810 and was carried on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>. In 1821 the royalist officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> joined the insurgents under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Mexican War of Independence" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hpav7 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Independencia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Mexican independence was not an inevitable outcome of the relationship between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and its American-born Spaniards, the criollos. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> of the mid-eighteenth century aimed to strengthen the Spanish state, weaken the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and place peninsular-born officials in office, and many historians see them as hastening the breakdown of imperial unity. An unofficial racial hierarchy kept criollos out of the highest offices, though most did not wish to overturn the social order entirely.</p><p>The immediate trigger was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1808, which deposed Charles IV and put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> on the throne. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> the city council promoted autonomy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, but on the night of 15 September 1808 a group led by the landowner Gabriel de Yermo overthrew the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-iturrigaray">José de Iturrigaray</a>, fearing he might make himself head of a new state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first phase began on 16 September 1810, when the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla</a> issued the Cry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a>. He drew tens of thousands of ordinary men, but did not organise them into a disciplined force. Hidalgo was captured, defrocked and executed in July 1811. The insurgency had already spread south, where the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> led a second phase, organising his forces, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics and producing a written statement of aims, the Sentimientos de la Nación of 1813. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> was captured and executed in December 1815.</p><p>After that, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a> became the most important leader, and from 1815 to 1821 most of the fighting was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare in the hot country of the south. From 1816 to 1820 the war was a stalemate: the royal army held the major cities, but whole stretches of countryside were not pacified.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The decisive change came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1820 a military coup there forced Ferdinand VII to restore the liberal Constitution of 1812. Conservative royalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, seeing it as a threat, came to regard independence as a way of keeping their position. The royalist officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>, and many royalist commanders and soldiers changed sides. On 24 August 1821 Iturbide and the new viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-odonoju">Juan O'Donojú</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a>, which the Spanish government refused to accept.</p><p>The Army of the Three Guarantees entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 27 September 1821, and independence was proclaimed the next day. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a> under Iturbide gave way in 1823 to a federal republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised Mexican independence on 28 December 1836, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> the first former colony it recognised. Mexicans later marked both 16 September, for Hidalgo's call, and 27 September, for actual independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mexican War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The province suffered in the <b>Mexican War of Independence</b>, including a republican revolt crushed at the Medina River in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-texas">Spanish Texas</a></li></ul></section>
